Subgroup Metrics: Implement custom functions to calculate Subgroup AUC and False Negative Rates (FNR) specifically for the subset of data where specific identity columns (the identity_probs tensor) are present. This is the core of your bias evaluation.
3. Training Loop and Optimization
Model Training: Write the PyTorch (or Hugging Face Trainer) training loop. You will need to define the optimizer (e.g., AdamW), learning rate scheduler, and cross-entropy loss.
Validation Loop: Integrate your custom metrics (from step 2) into the validation loop so you can monitor both general accuracy and bias gaps during training.
Model Checkpointing: Implement a mechanism to save the best model weights based on validation performance, while being mindful of the persistent storage limits mentioned in the syllabus.
4. Experiment Execution and Reproducibility
Hyperparameter Management: Set up a clean way to manage seeds, batch sizes, and learning rates (to fulfill the "reproducibility" grading criteria in the Assignment).
Sanity Checking: As required by the assignment guidelines, create a small script or flag to overfit the model on a tiny sample (e.g., 50 examples) to mathematically ensure the training loop has no bugs before kicking off a 3-hour run.
Execution: Run the fine-tuning on the university's Slurm GPU cluster.
